.TH VCLOCK 1 "2026-09-10" "vclock 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vclock \- transparent analog desktop clock
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vclock
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vclock
draws an analog clock on the desktop with no window frame and a transparent
background, so that only the clock face itself is visible.  Faces, colours,
hands and marks are configurable for each clock, and any number of clocks can
be shown at once.
.PP
The clock has no title bar.  Drag it with the left mouse button to move it,
hold
.B Ctrl
and turn the mouse wheel to resize it, and click the right mouse button for a
menu with Settings, the clock list, help and About on it.
.PP
Starting
.B vclock
while it is already running does not start a second copy: the second launch
hands over what it was asked for and stops, and the copy already running does
it.  Instances are told apart by their configuration directory.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fINAME\fR
Read and write \fINAME\fR instead of the default configuration.  A bare name is
a file in the configuration directory, so
.B world
means \fIworld.cfg\fR; anything with a
.B /
in it is a path of your own.  Repeat the option to run several clocks at once,
one for each configuration.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-daemon
Put the clocks up and nothing else.  This is what starting at login does, where
a window asking to be dealt with is the last thing wanted.
.TP
.B \-\-manage
Put the clocks up and open the clock list with them.  This is the default, so
the option is only needed to say so explicitly.  Giving it together with
.B \-\-daemon
is an error.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the options and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help\-all
Show the same summary with the Qt options included.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/vclock/
Configuration directory.  Each clock is one \fI.cfg\fR file, and
\fIvclocks.cfg\fR lists the clocks and which of them start automatically.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Run the default clock:
.B vclock
.TP
Run two named clocks at once:
.B vclock \-c desk \-c wall
.TP
Put the clocks up without opening the clock list:
.B vclock \-\-daemon
.TP
Open the clock list on the copy already running:
.B vclock \-\-manage
.SH SEE ALSO
Full documentation at:
.UR https://github.com/wryan/vclock
.UE
.SH AUTHOR
wryan
